<section class="col-xs-12 col-sm-12 col-md-12 col-lg-9 col-xl-9" id="main">
<div class="html" id="tag">
<h3 class="tex-subsection" id="subsection:uniqueness-factorization"><span data-tag="04PQ">9.6.7</span> Uniqueness of Factorizations</h3>
<p>
Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $S_{L}$ and $S_{R}$ be collections of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, and let $f: X \rightarrow Z$ be a morphism which factors as a composition 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  X \xrightarrow { f_{L} } Y \xrightarrow { f_{R} } Z \]
</div>
<p>
 where $f_{L}$ belongs to $S_{L}$ and $f_{R}$ belongs to $S_{R}$. Our goal in this section is to show that, if $S_{L}$ is left orthogonal to $S_{R}$, then this factorization is essentially unique (Theorem <a data-tag="04QC" href="/tag/04QC">9.6.7.2</a>). 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-notation" id="04PS">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04PS">Notation <span data-tag="04PS">9.6.7.1</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $S_{L}$ and $S_{R}$ be collections of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. We let $\operatorname{Fun}_{L}(\Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ denote the full subcategory of $\operatorname{Fun}(\Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ spanned by those diagrams </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@C =50pt@R=50pt{ &amp;  Y \ar [dr]^{f_{R}} &amp;  \\ X \ar [ur]^{ f_{L} } \ar [rr]^-{ f } &amp;  &amp;  Z } \]
</div>
<p> where $f_{L}$ belongs to $S_{L}$, and $\operatorname{Fun}_{R}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ the full subcategory of $\operatorname{Fun}(\Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ spanned by those diagrams where $f_{R}$ belongs to $S_{R}$. We let $\operatorname{Fun}_{LR}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ denote the intersection $\operatorname{Fun}_{L}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \cap \operatorname{Fun}_{R}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
We can now formulate our main result. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-theorem" id="04QC">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04QC">Theorem <span data-tag="04QC">9.6.7.2</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $S_{L}$ and $S_{R}$ be collections of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. If $S_{L}$ is left orthogonal to $S_{R}$, then the restriction functor </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  D: \operatorname{Fun}_{LR}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \quad \quad \sigma \mapsto d^{2}_{1}(\sigma ) \]
</div>
<p> is fully faithful. The converse holds if $S_{L}$ and $S_{R}$ contain all identity morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
Our proof of Theorem <a data-tag="04QC" href="/tag/04QC">9.6.7.2</a> will use the following consequence of Theorem <a data-tag="06WJ" href="/tag/06WJ">9.6.6.19</a>: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="06WS">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WS">Lemma <span data-tag="06WS">9.6.7.3</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $g: X \rightarrow Y$ be a morphism of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, so that the composition </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^1 \xrightarrow {(i,j) \mapsto ij} \Delta ^1 \xrightarrow {g} \operatorname{\mathcal{C}} \]
</div>
<p> determines a commutative diagram $\sigma :$ </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ X \ar [d]^{ \operatorname{id}_{X} } \ar [r]^-{ \operatorname{id}_{X} } &amp;  X \ar [d]^{g} \\ X \ar [r]^-{g} &amp;  Y } \]
</div>
<p> which we regard as a morphism from $\operatorname{id}_{X}$ to $g$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. Then a morphism $f: A \rightarrow B$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is left orthogonal to $g$ if and only if composition with $\sigma $ induces a homotopy equivalence of morphism spaces $\phi : \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_{X} ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, g)$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $_{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g}$ be the Kan complex described in Notation <a data-tag="06WE" href="/tag/06WE">9.6.6.16</a>. By virtue of Theorem <a data-tag="06WJ" href="/tag/06WJ">9.6.6.19</a>, $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$ if and only if the restriction map $\theta : _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g} \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}(f,g)$ is a homotopy equivalence. Unwinding the definitions, we see that $\theta $ factors as a composition 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g} \xrightarrow {\theta '} \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_ X, g )_{\sigma } \xrightarrow {\theta ''} \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, g), \]
</div>
<p>
       where $\theta '$ is given by precomposition with the map of simplicial sets 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^2 \rightarrow \Delta ^3 \quad \quad (i,j) \mapsto \begin{cases}  i &amp;  \text{if $j=0$} \\  2 &amp;  \text{if $j=1$} \\  i+2 &amp;  \text{if $j=2$.} \end{cases} \]
</div>
<p>
       By definition, the map $\phi $ is obtained by composing $\theta ''$ with a section of the restriction map $\rho : \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_ X, g )_{\sigma } \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_ X)$ (which is a trivial Kan fibration by virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="01PK" href="/tag/01PK">4.6.8.4</a>). Consequently, to prove Lemma <a data-tag="06WS" href="/tag/06WS">9.6.7.3</a>, it will suffice to show that $\theta '$ is a homotopy equivalence, or equivalently that the composition $\rho \circ \theta '$ is a homotopy equivalence. Since the constant map $\Delta ^1 \rightarrow \{ X\}  \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is right Kan extended from the vertex $\{ 1\}  \subseteq \Delta ^1$, evaluation at $1$ induces a trivial Kan fibration $\psi : \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_ X) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( B, X )$ (Corollary <a data-tag="030M" href="/tag/030M">7.3.6.9</a>). We are therefore reduced to showing that the composite map 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g} \xrightarrow {\theta '} \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_ X, g )_{\sigma } \xrightarrow {\rho } \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_ X) \xrightarrow {\psi } \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(B, X ) \]
</div>
<p>
       is a homotopy equivalence, which follows immediately from Proposition <a data-tag="01PK" href="/tag/01PK">4.6.8.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></p></p></p></article>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="06WT">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WT">Proposition <span data-tag="06WT">9.6.7.4</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing $2$-simplices $\sigma $ and $\sigma '$, which we depict as </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ &amp;  Y \ar [dr]^{g} &amp;  &amp;  &amp;  Y' \ar [dr]^{g'} &amp;  \\ X \ar [ur]^{f} \ar [rr]^{h} &amp;  &amp;  Z &amp;  X' \ar [ur]^{f'} \ar [rr]^{h'} &amp;  &amp;  Z'. } \]
</div>
<p> If $f$ is left orthogonal to $g'$, then the restriction map </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \theta : \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , \sigma ' ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( h, h' ) \]
</div>
<p> is a homotopy equivalence. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $T: \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^2 \rightarrow \Delta ^2$ denote the morphism of simplicial sets given on vertices by the formula 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ T(i,j,k) = \begin{cases}  i &amp;  \text{ if $k = 0$} \\  j+1 &amp;  \text{ if $k=1$} \\  2 &amp;  \text{ if $k=2$.} \end{cases} \]
</div>
<p>
       Then $(\sigma ' \circ T): \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^2 \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can be identified with a commutative diagram in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$, which we depict as 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \sigma ' \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  s^{1}_{0}(g') \ar [d] \\ s^{1}_{1}(h') \ar [r] &amp;  s_{1}^{1}(g'). } \]
</div>
<p>
       This diagram is a levelwise pullback square, and therefore determines a pullback diagram of morphism spaces 
    <p>
<div class="equation" id="06WU">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="06WU" href="/tag/06WU">9.46</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:annoying-proof} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , \sigma ') \ar [r] \ar [d]^{ \widetilde{\theta } } &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , s^{1}_{0}(g') ) \ar [d]^{\widetilde{\theta }_0} \\ \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , s^{1}_{1}(h') ) \ar [r]&amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , s_{1}^{1}(g') ) } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p>
       (Proposition <a data-tag="039L" href="/tag/039L">7.4.1.22</a>). Using Corollary <a data-tag="046J" href="/tag/046J">8.3.5.8</a>, we see that $\widetilde{\theta }$ fits into a commutative diagram 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , \sigma ') \ar [r] \ar [d]^{ \widetilde{\theta } } \ar [r]^-{\theta } \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( h, h') \ar [d]^{\operatorname{id}} \\ \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , s^{1}_{1}(h') ) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( h, h'), } \]
</div>
<p>
       where the right vertical map is an identity morphism and the lower horizontal map is a homotopy equivalence by virtue of Corollary <a data-tag="030M" href="/tag/030M">7.3.6.9</a> (since the degenerate $2$-simplex $s^{1}_{1}(h')$ is right Kan extended from the full subcategory $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \{  0 &lt; 2 \}  ) \subset \Delta ^2$). Consequently, the morphism $\theta $ is a homotopy equivalence if and only if $\widetilde{\theta }$ is a homotopy equivalence. Since the diagram (<a data-tag="06WU" href="/tag/06WU">9.46</a>) is a pullback square, it will suffice to show that $\widetilde{\theta }_0$ is a homotopy equivalence (Corollary <a data-tag="048F" href="/tag/048F">7.6.2.39</a>). We may therefore replace $\sigma '$ by $s^{1}_{0}(g')$, and thereby reduce to the case where $\sigma '$ is left-degenerate (Example <a data-tag="04ZP" href="/tag/04ZP">1.1.2.8</a>). Similarly, we may replace $\sigma $ by $s^{1}_{1}(f)$ and thereby reduce to the case where $\sigma $ is right-degenerate. In particular, we may assume that $\sigma $ is left Kan extended from the full subcategory $\Delta ^1 \subset \Delta ^2$. Using Corollary <a data-tag="046J" href="/tag/046J">8.3.5.8</a> again, we obtain a commutative diagram 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , \sigma ') \ar [r] \ar [d]^{ \widetilde{\theta } } \ar [r]^-{\theta } \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, f' ) \ar [d]^{\operatorname{id}} \\ \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , s^{1}_{1}(h') ) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, h'), } \]
</div>
<p>
       where the horizontal maps are homotopy equivalences (Corollary <a data-tag="030M" href="/tag/030M">7.3.6.9</a>). Since $\sigma '$ is left-degenerate, we have $f' = \operatorname{id}_{Y'}$ and $h' = g'$. We are therefore reduced to proving that the natural map $\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, \operatorname{id}_{Y'} ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, g' )$ is a homotopy equivalence, which is a reformulation of our assumption that $f$ is left orthogonal to $g'$ (Lemma <a data-tag="06WS" href="/tag/06WS">9.6.7.3</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></article>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="06WV">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WV">Remark <span data-tag="06WV">9.6.7.5</span>.</a> In the situation of Proposition <a data-tag="06WT" href="/tag/06WT">9.6.7.4</a>, suppose that $\sigma = s_{1}^{1}(f)$ is left-degenerate and that $\sigma ' = s^{1}_{0}( g' )$ is right-degenerate (or, more generally, that $f'$ and $g$ are isomorphisms). In this case, the reduction steps in the proof are unnecessary, and the argument is reversible: $f$ is left orthogonal to $g'$ if and only if the restriction map $\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , \sigma ' ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( h, h' )$ is a homotopy equivalence. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof of Theorem <a data-tag="04QC" href="/tag/04QC">9.6.7.2</a>.</strong>
       Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $S_{L}$ and $S_{R}$ be collections of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Suppose first that $S_{L}$ is left orthogonal to $S_{R}$. In this case, Proposition <a data-tag="06WT" href="/tag/06WT">9.6.7.4</a> guarantees that the restriction map 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \theta : \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }(\sigma , \sigma ') \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}(\Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( d^{2}_{1}(\sigma ), d^{2}_{1}(\sigma ') ) \]
</div>
<p>
       is a homotopy equivalence whenever $\sigma $ belongs to $\operatorname{Fun}_{L}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ and $\sigma '$ belongs to $\operatorname{Fun}_{R}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. It follows that the functor 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  D: \operatorname{Fun}_{LR}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \quad \quad \sigma \mapsto d^{2}_{1}(\sigma ) \]
</div>
<p>
       is fully faithful. 
    <p>
      We now prove the converse. Assume that $D$ is fully faithful and that $S_{L}$ and $S_{R}$ contain all identity morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Take $f \in S_ L$ and $g \in S_ R$, and let $\widetilde{f} = s^{1}_{1}(f)$ and $\widetilde{g} = s^{1}_{0}(g)$ denote the degenerate $2$-simplices of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ appearing in the proof of Corollary <a data-tag="04QE" href="/tag/04QE">9.6.8.9</a>. Since $S_{L}$ and $S_{R}$ contain all identity morphisms, we can view $\widetilde{f}$ and $\widetilde{g}$ as objects of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}_{LR}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. Our assumption that $D$ is fully faithful guarantees that the Kan fibration $\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \widetilde{f}, \widetilde{g} ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f,g )$ is a homotopy equivalence, so that $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$ by virtue of Remark <a data-tag="06WV" href="/tag/06WV">9.6.7.5</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></p></p></article>
